- Join OCRA
- Help develop the sport locally


Supporting New Athletes

Every experienced OCR athlete had a first race.

OCRA wants the Irish obstacle racing community to remain welcoming to people who are discovering the sport for the first time.

You do not need to be an elite athlete, complete every obstacle or arrive with years of training behind you.

There should be a place within the sport for people who want to compete seriously and for people who simply enjoy training, racing and being part of the community.


Clubs and Local Communities

Local clubs and training groups are at the heart of sustainable sporting development.

They provide regular opportunities for people to train, learn from each other and become involved beyond individual race weekends.

OCRA wants to help establish stronger links between clubs throughout Ireland and support the creation of new communities in areas where OCR is still developing.


Events Bring the Community Together

Independent race organisers play an important role in the Irish OCR community.

Events such as Hard as Oak, Rampage, Hell & Back, Spartan and Turf Warrior provide opportunities for thousands of people to experience obstacle racing in different formats.

OCRA wants to work constructively with event organisers while continuing to develop national competition, qualification and athlete pathways.


Volunteering

Obstacle sport depends heavily on volunteers.

Volunteers support:

- Competitions
- Clubs
- Community activities
- Technical operations
- Athlete support
- Event delivery
- OCRA programmes

Volunteering can also provide a pathway into coaching, Technical Officiating, event organisation and other roles within the sport.


